{
  "id": "DrugBank_DB01176 - MONDO_6004",
  "verbalization": "Cyclizine, a centrally acting antihistamine, is linked to Vasomotor Rhinitis through a series of indirect pathways. The evidence suggests that Cyclizine may act as a mediator in the development of Vasomotor Rhinitis by influencing inflammatory processes through its indication of Papillary Conjunctivitis and Olopatadine. Olopatadine, a second-generation antihistamine, is also indicated for Vasomotor Rhinitis, and its relationship with Cyclizine implies a potential shared mechanism in modulating inflammatory responses. The indirect pathways highlight the role of antihistamines in triggering or exacerbating vasomotor symptoms, possibly through effects on mast cell degranulation or histamine receptor activation. The cumulative evidence supports a mechanistic link between Cyclizine and Vasomotor Rhinitis, though the exact nature of the interaction remains to be elucidated.",
